  "body": "## Why\n\nThe force-composed opacity (ADR-0002, merged in #2) turned out to be wrong and harmful:\n- **Visible regression:** 0.99 opacity over the transparent host window let ~1% of the desktop bleed through the video.\n- **Doesn't fix the bug:** the flash occurs when shows is **pinned**, where the video is *already* stably `Composed: Flip` yet still flashes — so pinning its own plane changes nothing. The earlier \"confirmed\" capture was unpinned (the titlebar offset, not the opacity, made it Composed). The flash is uptime-correlated and reproduces with no game present.\n\n## Changes\n- **Revert** the DComp opacity effect on the video visual — video is opaque again (ADR-0003 supersedes ADR-0002; shell-contract note dropped).\n- **Add observability** for the ongoing investigation (to `%APPDATA%\\shows\\shows.log`, prefix `OBS `): ~1Hz heartbeat (foreground window class + on_top/fullscreen/standby), WM_ACTIVATE/WM_ACTIVATEAPP focus edges, WM_DISPLAYCHANGE, and a global **Ctrl+Alt+F** flash marker. Pairs with continuous system-wide PresentMon + a resource monitor.\n\n## Status\nThe flash is **not yet fixed** — this stops chasing a refuted theory and instruments the real one. Research finding: a DP/DSC link re-train isn't directly observable from user space, so the rig fingerprints the OS event that accompanies it and correlates on QPC.\n\nSquash-merge recommended (history still contains the old diagnostic/revert commits).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
